Worked example
The lab’s nginx.conf.j2:
# {{ ansible_managed }}
worker_processes {{ lemonade_nginx_workers }};
server {
    listen 80;
    location / { proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:{{ lemonade_app_port }}; }
}
And index.html.j2, with a loop:
&lt;h1&gt;Sunny Lemonade Stand&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
{% for drink in lemonade_menu %}
  &lt;li&gt;{{ drink.name }}: ${{ '%.2f' | format(drink.price) }}&lt;/li&gt;
{% endfor %}
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Served by {{ inventory_hostname }}, version {{ lemonade_version }}&lt;/p&gt;
After ansible-playbook site.yml, web1 has:
# Ansible managed
worker_processes 4;
server {
    listen 80;
    location / { proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080; }
}
&lt;h1&gt;Sunny Lemonade Stand&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;Classic Lemonade: $2.50&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;Pink Lemonade: $3.00&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;Iced Tea: $2.00&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Served by web1, version 1.0.0&lt;/p&gt;
There are no blank lines where the {% for %} and {% endfor %} tags were, because trim_blocks is on by default.

Mathematical Abstraction

1. Three definitions as logic
Let \(C\) be the set of all changes and, for a change \(c \in C\):

\(\operatorname{pass}(c)\): the pipeline is green for \(c\);
\(\operatorname{releasable}(c)\): \(c\) is packaged and could go to production with one action;
\(\operatorname{deployed}(c)\): \(c\) is running in production.

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Continuous delivery:}\quad & \forall c \in C:\ \operatorname{pass}(c) \Rightarrow \operatorname{releasable}(c) \\
\text{Continuous deployment:}\quad & \forall c \in C:\ \operatorname{pass}(c) \Rightarrow \operatorname{deployed}(c)
\end{aligned}
\]
where

\(\forall c \in C\) reads “for every change \(c\) in the set \(C\)”;
\(\Rightarrow\) reads “implies”: whenever the left side is true, the right side must be true too.

Example. On Tuesday the team merges 5 changes and all 5 are green. With continuous delivery, all 5 must be releasable: \(\operatorname{releasable}(c_1) = \dots = \operatorname{releasable}(c_5) = \text{true}\). Only 2 of them are deployed that day, because the reviewer approves twice. That is fine for delivery. It would break continuous deployment, which requires \(\operatorname{deployed}(c_i) = \text{true}\) for all 5.


2. A simple model of why long branches hurt
This is a simplified model, not a law. Assume \(n\) developers each change about \(r\) files per day, chosen at random from a codebase of \(F\) files, and they integrate every \(T\) days. Two developers’ work overlaps when they touch the same file. The expected number of such overlapping files, summed over all pairs of developers, is about
\[
E(T) \approx \binom{n}{2} \cdot \frac{(rT)^2}{F}
\]
where

\(n\) is the number of developers (a whole number);
\(\binom{n}{2} = \frac{n(n-1)}{2}\) is the number of pairs of developers;
\(r\) is files changed per developer per day;
\(T\) is the integration interval in days;
\(rT\) is how many files one developer has changed by merge time;
\(\frac{(rT)^2}{F}\) is the expected number of files two developers share: each of the \(rT\) files of the first developer is in the second developer’s set with probability \(\frac{rT}{F}\) (this needs \(rT\) much smaller than \(F\));
\(E(T)\) is the expected number of overlapping files, a rough count of merge-conflict risk.

Example. Ana, Ben and Caro plus one more friend: \(n = 4\), so \(\binom{4}{2} = \frac{4 \cdot 3}{2} = 6\) pairs. Each changes \(r = 3\) files a day in a codebase of \(F = 200\) files.

Integrating daily, \(T = 1\): \(E(1) \approx 6 \cdot \frac{(3 \cdot 1)^2}{200} = 6 \cdot \frac{9}{200} = 0.27\) overlapping files.
Integrating every 10 days, \(T = 10\): \(E(10) \approx 6 \cdot \frac{(3 \cdot 10)^2}{200} = 6 \cdot \frac{900}{200} = 27\) overlapping files.

The interval grew \(10\times\), but the overlap grew \(\frac{27}{0.27} = 100\times\), because \(T\) is squared. Real files are not chosen at random (some files, like server.mjs, are touched by everyone), so real numbers differ, but the lesson holds: conflict risk grows faster than the waiting time, which is the reasoning behind “if it hurts, do it more often”.

Mathematical Abstraction
Database connections add up across all Cloud Run instances.
\[
n_{\max} \times k \le C_{\max}
\]
where:

\(n_{\max}\) is Cloud Run’s --max-instances;
\(k\) is the pool size per instance (the max option in the code above);
\(n_{\max} \times k\) is the most connections the app can open at once;
\(C_{\max}\) is the database’s connection limit (SHOW max_connections;), minus a few kept free for admins;
\(\le\) says the left side must not exceed the right side.

Example: \(n_{\max} = 10\) and \(k = 5\) give \(10 \times 5 = 50\) connections. If \(C_{\max} = 100\), then \(50 \le 100\) holds, with room to spare. Remove --max-instances and a traffic spike could start 100 instances: \(100 \times 5 = 500 &gt; 100\), and new customers would get too many clients.
How much data can you lose? The worst case is the time since the last restore point.
\[
L_{\max} = \begin{cases}
\Delta_{\text{backup}} & \text{without point-in-time recovery} \\
\Delta_{\text{log}} & \text{with point-in-time recovery}
\end{cases}
\]
where:

\(L_{\max}\) is the largest amount of recent work, measured in time, that a restore could lose (the recovery point objective);
\(\Delta_{\text{backup}}\) is the time between automated backups;
\(\Delta_{\text{log}}\) is how far the archived write-ahead log lags behind the live database, usually seconds to a few minutes.

Example: with daily backups at 03:00 and no point-in-time recovery, \(\Delta_{\text{backup}} = 24\) hours. A bad migration at 02:55 the next night loses up to \(24\,\text{h} - 5\,\text{min} = 23\,\text{h}\,55\,\text{min}\) of orders. With point-in-time recovery, you restore to 02:54 and lose only the last minute or so.
Will Firestore stay free? Daily reads are visits times reads per visit.
\[
R = v \times r \le 50{,}000
\]
where:

\(v\) is the number of page views per day;
\(r\) is the number of documents each page view reads;
\(R\) is the total reads per day;
\(50{,}000\) is the Standard edition’s free daily read allowance (at the time of writing).

Example: the menu page reads 2 documents (today’s counter and the specials), so \(r = 2\). With \(v = 12{,}000\) views, \(R = 12{,}000 \times 2 = 24{,}000 \le 50{,}000\): free. On a festival day, \(v = 30{,}000\) gives \(R = 60{,}000\), which is \(10{,}000\) reads over. Caching the counter for 10 seconds would bring it back under.

The problem
The owner has the lemonade-stand container image from Part 3. They want it online with:

no VM to patch and no cluster to run;
enough copies for the lunchtime rush, and zero cost at 3 a.m.;
a safe way to try a new recipe page on a few customers before everyone sees it.

Mathematical Abstraction
How many instances? As a first approximation, Cloud Run needs enough instances to hold all in-flight requests, within the limits you set.
\[
N \approx \min\Big( \max\Big( \Big\lceil \frac{R}{c} \Big\rceil,\ n_{\min} \Big),\ n_{\max} \Big)
\]
where:

\(R\) is the number of requests in flight at the same moment;
\(c\) is the concurrency setting (--concurrency), the most requests per instance;
\(\lceil \cdot \rceil\) rounds up, because a half-full instance is still a whole instance;
\(n_{\min}\) and \(n_{\max}\) are --min-instances and --max-instances;
\(\max(\cdot, n_{\min})\) keeps at least the minimum warm, and \(\min(\cdot, n_{\max})\) never goes above the cap;
\(N\) is the number of instances.

Example: settings \(c = 80\), \(n_{\min} = 0\), \(n_{\max} = 10\).

3 a.m., \(R = 0\): \(\lceil 0/80 \rceil = 0\), then \(\max(0, 0) = 0\), then \(\min(0, 10) = 0\) instances. Cost: zero.
Lunch rush, \(R = 200\): \(\lceil 200/80 \rceil = \lceil 2.5 \rceil = 3\), then \(\max(3, 0) = 3\) and \(\min(3, 10) = 3\) instances.
A viral post, \(R = 1000\): \(\lceil 1000/80 \rceil = \lceil 12.5 \rceil = 13\), but \(\min(13, 10) = 10\). The cap holds; the extra requests wait in a queue and may time out.

This is an idealised lower bound, not Google’s exact algorithm. The real autoscaler also watches CPU use and aims to keep instances below their maximum concurrency, so it often adds instances earlier.
Traffic splitting divides requests by percentage.
\[
T_i = T \times \frac{w_i}{100}, \qquad \sum_i w_i = 100
\]
where:

\(T\) is the total number of requests in a period;
\(w_i\) is the percentage assigned to revision \(i\);
\(\sum_i w_i = 100\) says the percentages must add up to exactly 100;
\(T_i\) is the expected number of requests revision \(i\) receives.

Example: with \(T = 5000\) requests in the lunch hour and the canary at \(w = 10\), the canary gets \(5000 \times 10/100 = 500\) requests and the stable revision gets \(5000 \times 90/100 = 4500\). If v2 has a bug that fails every request, \(500\) customers see it, not \(5000\).
Cost in request-based billing counts resource-seconds above the monthly free allowance.
\[
\text{cost} = (V - V_0)^{+} \, p_V + (G - G_0)^{+} \, p_G + (Q - Q_0)^{+} \, p_Q
\]
where:

\(t\) is the billable instance time in seconds, counted while an instance has at least one request in flight;
\(V = t \times \text{vCPUs}\) is the vCPU-seconds and \(G = t \times \text{GiB}\) the GiB-seconds of memory;
\(Q\) is the number of requests;
\(V_0, G_0, Q_0\) are the free monthly allowances;
\(p_V, p_G, p_Q\) are the prices per vCPU-second, per GiB-second and per request;
\((x)^{+}\) means \(\max(x, 0)\): below the free allowance you pay nothing.

Example: at the time of writing, Tier 1 request-based prices are \(p_V = 0.000024\) and \(p_G = 0.0000025\) dollars, \(p_Q = 0.40\) dollars per million requests, with free allowances \(V_0 = 180{,}000\), \(G_0 = 360{,}000\) and \(Q_0 = 2{,}000{,}000\) per month (check the pricing page). The Lemonade Stand is busy for \(t = 360{,}000\) s (100 hours) a month, with 1 vCPU and 0.5 GiB, serving \(Q = 1{,}500{,}000\) requests.

\(V = 360{,}000 \times 1 = 360{,}000\), so \((360{,}000 - 180{,}000) \times 0.000024 = 180{,}000 \times 0.000024 = 4.32\) dollars.
\(G = 360{,}000 \times 0.5 = 180{,}000\), which is below \(G_0\), so \(0\).
\(Q = 1.5\) million, which is below \(Q_0\), so \(0\).

Total: about \(4.32\) dollars a month. Concurrency is why it is so cheap: 80 overlapping requests on one instance count as one instance-second, not 80.

The problem
Last Saturday the order form returned errors for two hours, and the owner found out from an angry customer. They want:

to see what the app printed when it failed;
to be woken up when the menu is down, not every time one request fails;
a fair rule for when to ship new features and when to stop and fix reliability;
an email before the monthly bill passes $20.

Mathematical Abstraction
The SLI is a ratio of good events to valid events.
\[
\text{SLI} = \frac{g}{n}
\]
where:

\(n\) is the number of valid requests in the window (excluding, for example, health checks from your own monitors);
\(g\) is the number of those that were good: answered without a 5xx error;
\(\text{SLI}\) is a number between 0 and 1, often written as a percentage.

Example: in October the Lemonade Stand served \(n = 1{,}000{,}000\) requests, of which \(800\) failed, so \(g = 999{,}200\). Then \(\text{SLI} = 999{,}200 / 1{,}000{,}000 = 0.9992 = 99.92\%\).
The error budget is the failure the SLO allows.
\[
B = (1 - s) \times n \qquad \text{or, in time,} \qquad B_t = (1 - s) \times W
\]
where:

\(s\) is the SLO as a fraction, such as \(0.999\);
\(1 - s\) is the allowed failure fraction;
\(n\) is the number of valid requests, so \(B\) counts the failed requests you may have;
\(W\) is the window length in minutes, so \(B_t\) counts the minutes of downtime you may have.

Example: with \(s = 0.999\) and \(n = 1{,}000{,}000\), \(B = 0.001 \times 1{,}000{,}000 = 1{,}000\) failed requests. October used \(800\), so \(1{,}000 - 800 = 200\) were left: the shop could keep shipping features. In time, a 30-day window is \(W = 30 \times 24 \times 60 = 43{,}200\) minutes, so \(B_t = 0.001 \times 43{,}200 = 43.2\) minutes of full outage per month. Last Saturday’s two-hour outage (\(120\) minutes) would have spent the budget almost three times over: \(120 / 43.2 \approx 2.8\).
The burn rate says how fast the budget is going.
\[
b = \frac{e}{1 - s}, \qquad T_{\text{empty}} = \frac{W}{b}
\]
where:

\(e\) is the error fraction measured right now, over a short recent period;
\(b\) is the burn rate: \(b = 1\) means the budget would last exactly the whole window, and \(b &gt; 1\) means it runs out early;
\(W\) is the SLO window (30 days);
\(T_{\text{empty}}\) is how long until the budget is gone if the current error rate continues.

Example: during an incident, \(e = 0.005\) (0.5% of requests fail). Then \(b = 0.005 / 0.001 = 5\) and \(T_{\text{empty}} = 30 / 5 = 6\) days. That is serious but not a 3 a.m. emergency, so an email is enough. In a full outage, \(e = 1\) gives \(b = 1 / 0.001 = 1000\) and \(T_{\text{empty}} = 43{,}200 / 1000 = 43.2\) minutes: wake someone up. Google’s SRE Workbook suggests paging when \(b \ge 14.4\) for an hour. That burn uses \(14.4 \times 1 / 720 = 0.02\), or 2%, of a 720-hour budget in that single hour.

Mathematical Abstraction
The hierarchy is a tree, so every resource has exactly one parent, and the chain of parents ends at the top. That chain is the resource’s path.
\[
\operatorname{path}(r) = \big( r,\ \operatorname{parent}(r),\ \operatorname{parent}(\operatorname{parent}(r)),\ \dots \big)
\]
where:

\(r\) is any resource, folder or project;
\(\operatorname{parent}(r)\) is the container directly above \(r\) (a project’s parent is a folder or the organization; a project with no organization has no parent);
the list stops when it reaches a node with no parent;
\(\operatorname{path}(r)\) is the whole chain from \(r\) up to the top.

Example: \(\operatorname{path}(\texttt{lemonade-web}) = (\texttt{lemonade-web},\ \texttt{lemonade-stand-dev},\ \texttt{shop},\ \texttt{example.com})\). That is 4 nodes: the service, its project, its folder and the organization. Lesson 3 shows that permissions on any of these 4 nodes apply to the service.
Billing is a function from projects to billing accounts:
\[
\beta : P \to B \cup \lbrace \bot \rbrace, \qquad \operatorname{canPay}(p) \iff \beta(p) \neq \bot
\]
where:

\(P\) is the set of your projects and \(B\) is the set of billing accounts;
\(\beta(p)\) is the billing account linked to project \(p\);
\(\bot\) (“bottom”) stands for “no billing account linked”;
“\(\to\)” says that each project maps to exactly one value, so a project never has two billing accounts;
\(\operatorname{canPay}(p)\) is true when the project can use paid services.

Example: \(\beta(\texttt{lemonade-stand-dev}) = \texttt{01A2B3-C4D5E6-F7A8B9}\), which is not \(\bot\), so paid services work. A brand-new project lemonade-stand-test that nobody linked has \(\beta = \bot\), so gcloud services enable run.googleapis.com fails there. Two projects can map to the same account: \(\beta(\texttt{lemonade-stand-dev}) = \beta(\texttt{lemonade-stand-prod})\) is allowed.
The zone rule from earlier completes the picture: the tree decides who controls a resource, \(\beta\) decides who pays, and \(Z(x)\) decides which outages it survives.

The problem
The owner wants one classic web server, web1, in London. It should:

start nginx and show the menu by itself when it boots;
accept web traffic on port 80 from anyone;
block one troublesome address range that keeps spamming the order form;
accept SSH only through Google’s secure tunnel, never from the open internet.

Mathematical Abstraction
How many VMs fit in a subnet? A CIDR range /p fixes the first \(p\) bits of the 32-bit address and leaves the rest free.
\[
U = 2^{32 - p} - 4
\]
where:

\(p\) is the prefix length, the number after the slash (/24 gives \(p = 24\));
\(32 - p\) is the number of free bits in an IPv4 address;
\(2^{32-p}\) is how many addresses the range contains;
\(4\) is the number of addresses Google keeps in every primary range (network, gateway, second-to-last, broadcast);
\(U\) is the number of addresses you can give to VMs.

Example: lemonade-london is 10.10.0.0/24, so \(U = 2^{32-24} - 4 = 2^{8} - 4 = 256 - 4 = 252\) usable addresses. The smallest allowed subnet, /29, gives \(U = 2^{3} - 4 = 8 - 4 = 4\), which is enough for a tiny test.
Which firewall rule wins? For one packet \(t\), let \(M(t)\) be the set of rules that match it (direction, source, port, target). The implied deny rule always matches ingress, so \(M(t)\) is never empty.
\[
\begin{aligned}
q(t) &= \min_{f \in M(t)} \operatorname{prio}(f) \\
\operatorname{action}(t) &=
\begin{cases}
\text{deny} & \text{if some } f \in M(t) \text{ with } \operatorname{prio}(f) = q(t) \text{ says deny} \\
\text{allow} & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{aligned}
\]
where:

\(t\) is one incoming connection attempt;
\(M(t)\) is the set of firewall rules whose direction, source range, protocol, port and target all match \(t\);
\(\operatorname{prio}(f)\) is rule \(f\)’s priority number, from 0 to 65535;
\(q(t)\) is the smallest priority number among the matching rules, meaning the most important one;
if several rules share that number, a deny among them wins.

Example 1: a request from 203.0.113.7 to web1 on port 80. It matches lemonade-deny-spammers (900, deny), lemonade-allow-http (1000, allow) and the implied rule (65535, deny). Then \(q = \min(900, 1000, 65535) = 900\), and the rule at 900 says deny: blocked.
Example 2: a customer at 198.51.100.9 on port 80 matches lemonade-allow-http (1000) and the implied rule (65535). Then \(q = 1000\), and that rule says allow: allowed.
Example 3: the same customer tries port 5432 (Postgres). Only the implied rule matches, so \(q = 65535\), deny: blocked. That is why a closed-by-default network is safe.

The problem
The Lemonade Stand now has two projects: lemonade-stand-dev for experiments and lemonade-stand-prod for real customers. The owner wants to:

type commands instead of clicking through web pages, so the steps can be saved and repeated;
switch between dev and prod without retyping --project every time;
never run a delete in prod by accident.

Mathematical Abstraction
A gcloud command reads a list \(L\) of things from an API. --filter keeps a subset of \(L\), and --format turns each kept item into text.
\[
\operatorname{out} = \big\lbrace\, \operatorname{fmt}(x) \;:\; x \in L,\ \varphi(x) \,\big\rbrace
\]
where:

\(L\) is the full list the API returned (all configurations, all zones, all VMs);
\(x \in L\) means “\(x\) is one item of that list”;
\(\varphi(x)\) (“phi of \(x\)”) is the filter condition, true or false for each item;
\(\operatorname{fmt}(x)\) is the text --format prints for one item, for example only its name;
\(\operatorname{out}\) is what appears on your screen.

Example: in the worked example, \(L = \lbrace \texttt{default}, \texttt{lemonade-dev}, \texttt{lemonade-prod} \rbrace\) has \(3\) items. The condition \(\varphi(x)\) is “\(x\) is the active configuration”. It is true only for lemonade-dev, so 1 of the 3 items survives. \(\operatorname{fmt}\) prints only the name, so \(\operatorname{out} = \lbrace \texttt{lemonade-dev} \rbrace\): one line of text.
Together with the precedence rule \(v(k) = \operatorname{first}(F, E, C)\), this is all gcloud does: decide which project and region to use, call the API, then filter and format the answer.

Mathematical Abstraction
Lambda charges per request and per GB-second (memory multiplied by run time):
\[
C = N \cdot p_r + N \cdot t \cdot m \cdot p_d
\]
where:

\(C\) is the monthly cost in dollars;
\(N\) is the number of invocations in the month;
\(p_r\) is the price per request: $0.20 per million, so $0.0000002;
\(t\) is the run time of one call in seconds (billed rounded up to the next millisecond);
\(m\) is the configured memory in GB, where 128 MB \(= 128/1024 = 0.125\) GB;
\(p_d\) is the price per GB-second: $0.0000166667 in London for x86.

Example: count-orders runs once a day, \(N = 30\), for \(t = 0.2\) s with \(m = 0.125\) GB. Duration costs \(30 \times 0.2 \times 0.125 = 0.75\) GB-s, so \(0.75 \times 0.0000166667 = \$0.0000125\). Requests cost \(30 \times 0.0000002 = \$0.000006\). Together that is about $0.00002 a month. Even a busy menu API with \(N = 300{,}000\) calls of \(0.05\) s costs \(300{,}000 \times 0.0000002 + 300{,}000 \times 0.05 \times 0.125 \times 0.0000166667 = 0.06 + 0.031 = \$0.09\). Both fit inside the monthly free allowance of 1 million requests and 400,000 GB-seconds (prices at time of writing, November 2026).
How many robots are awake at once? In a steady stream of requests, Little’s law gives
\[
c = \lambda \cdot t
\]
where:

\(c\) is the average concurrency, the number of requests being handled at the same time;
\(\lambda\) (lambda) is the arrival rate in requests per second;
\(t\) is the average run time in seconds.

Example: at the lunchtime peak \(\lambda = 50\) requests per second and \(t = 0.2\) s, so \(c = 50 \times 0.2 = 10\), far below the default limit of 1,000. If a slow database makes each call take \(t = 2\) s, then \(c = 50 \times 2 = 100\). The same traffic now needs ten times as many environments, which is why a slow dependency can suddenly hit the concurrency limit. Little’s law holds for long-run averages in a stable system. It does not predict short bursts.

Explain it like I’m ten






TipOrdering a computer from a menu



Renting a server on EC2 is like ordering at a café. You choose:

the recipe: what is already on the computer’s disk when it starts, like a cake-mix box (the AMI);
the size: nano, micro, small, medium, large, just like T-shirt sizes (the instance type);
a key for the door (a key pair), and a door guard who decides who may knock (a security group);
a note for the first morning: “when you wake up, install nginx and put up the menu” (user data).

Stop puts the computer to sleep. You stop paying for the computer, but its disk is kept, and that disk still costs a little. Terminate gives it back to the café forever.
Werner Vogels’ fourth AWS lesson is automation is key: people should not have to log in and click things to set up a server (2016). The note for the first morning is exactly that kind of automation.

Mathematical Abstraction
Inside one family, each size step doubles the price (and here also the memory):
\[
r_k = r_0 \cdot 2^{k}
\]
where:

\(k\) counts size steps above the smallest size: nano \(= 0\), micro \(= 1\), small \(= 2\), medium \(= 3\), large \(= 4\);
\(r_0\) is the hourly price of the smallest size, in dollars per hour;
\(r_k\) is the hourly price \(k\) steps up.

Example: for t3 in London, \(r_0 = \$0.0059\) (t3.nano). Then t3.large is \(r_4 = 0.0059 \times 2^4 = 0.0059 \times 16 = \$0.0944\) per hour, exactly the published price. Memory follows the same rule, \(0.5 \times 2^4 = 8\) GiB. The pattern holds for these sizes. CPU does not always follow it: every size from nano to large has 2 vCPUs. So check the table before relying on it.
What a server costs in a month depends on its state:
\[
C(\text{state}) =
\begin{cases}
r \cdot h + s \cdot g & \text{running} \\
s \cdot g & \text{stopped} \\
0 & \text{terminated}
\end{cases}
\]
where:

\(C\) is the monthly cost in dollars;
\(r\) is the hourly instance price and \(h\) the hours it runs;
\(s\) is the disk price in dollars per GB-month (gp3 in London: $0.0928);
\(g\) is the disk size in GB;
the cases say: running pays for the computer and the disk; stopped pays only for the disk; terminated pays nothing.

Example: web1 (t3.micro, 8 GB gp3 disk) running all month costs \(0.0118 \times 730 + 0.0928 \times 8 = 8.614 + 0.742 = \$9.36\). Stopped all month it costs \(0.0928 \times 8 = \$0.74\). Terminated, $0. If it also had a public IPv4 address, add \(0.005 \times 730 = \$3.65\) in every state except terminated (prices at time of writing, November 2026).

Mathematical Abstraction
AWS decides every request with the same rule. Let a request \(q\) be a triple of principal, action and resource:
\[
\operatorname{decision}(q) =
\begin{cases}
\text{Deny} & \text{if } \exists\, s \in S_q : e(s) = \text{Deny} \\
\text{Allow} & \text{else if } \exists\, s \in S_q : e(s) = \text{Allow} \\
\text{Deny (implicit)} & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]
where:

\(q\) is one request, for example “menu-editor wants s3:DeleteObject on index.html”;
\(S_q\) is the set of policy statements whose Action, Resource and Condition all match \(q\);
\(s \in S_q\) means “\(s\) is one of those matching statements”, and \(\exists\) reads “there exists”;
\(e(s)\) is the Effect of statement \(s\), either Allow or Deny;
the cases are checked top to bottom: an explicit Deny always wins, then an Allow, and otherwise the default is Deny.

Example: the menu editor has one statement, EditTheMenuOnly.

\(q_1\) = s3:PutObject on index.html. \(S_{q_1} = \lbrace \texttt{EditTheMenuOnly} \rbrace\). No Deny, one Allow, so the result is Allow.
\(q_2\) = s3:DeleteObject on index.html. No statement mentions DeleteObject, so \(S_{q_2} = \emptyset\) and the result is Deny (implicit).
Add a second statement, NoMenuChanges, that denies s3:PutObject. Now \(S_{q_1} = \lbrace \texttt{EditTheMenuOnly}, \texttt{NoMenuChanges} \rbrace\). A Deny exists, so the first case fires and the result is Deny, even though an Allow also matches.

This is the single-account core of the rule. Real evaluation also checks organisation policies (SCPs), permission boundaries, session policies and resource-based policies, and every one of them can only add more ways to say Deny.
Least privilege compares what you granted with what the job needs:
\[
E = G \setminus N, \qquad \text{least privilege} \iff E = \emptyset
\]
where:

\(G\) is the set of (action, resource) pairs the policies grant;
\(N\) is the set of (action, resource) pairs the job really needs;
\(G \setminus N\) (“\(G\) minus \(N\)”) keeps the pairs in \(G\) that are not in \(N\);
\(E\) is the excess: permissions that can only cause harm;
\(\iff\) reads “exactly when”; \(\emptyset\) is the empty set.

Example: the bucket holds 3 objects (index.html, logo.png, prices.json), and we look at 3 actions (Get, Put, Delete). The editor needs \(N = \lbrace (\text{Get}, \texttt{index.html}), (\text{Put}, \texttt{index.html}) \rbrace\), so \(\lvert N \rvert = 2\). Attaching s3:* on the bucket grants at least \(3 \times 3 = 9\) pairs, so \(\lvert E \rvert = 9 - 2 = 7\) extra ways to break things, including deleting the logo. Our policy grants exactly \(G = N\), so \(E = \emptyset\) and \(\lvert E \rvert = 0\).
Finally, the CLI picks each setting (region, profile, credentials) from the first place that defines it:
\[
v = \operatorname{first}\left(v_{\text{flag}},\ v_{\text{env}},\ v_{\text{file}}\right)
\]
where:

\(v\) is the value the CLI finally uses, for example the region;
\(v_{\text{flag}}\) comes from a command-line option such as --region;
\(v_{\text{env}}\) comes from an environment variable such as AWS_REGION;
\(v_{\text{file}}\) comes from ~/.aws/config or ~/.aws/credentials;
\(\operatorname{first}(\dots)\) returns the leftmost value that is set and skips missing ones.

Example: ~/.aws/config says region = eu-west-2, but an old shell still has AWS_REGION=us-east-1, and you type no --region. Then \(v = \operatorname{first}(\text{missing}, \texttt{us-east-1}, \texttt{eu-west-2}) = \texttt{us-east-1}\). Your new server appears in Virginia, not London. aws configure list shows this immediately.

Mathematical Abstraction
A CIDR block with prefix length \(p\) contains
\[
N = 2^{\,32 - p}, \qquad U = N - 5
\]
where:

\(N\) is the total number of IPv4 addresses in the block;
\(p\) is the prefix length, the number after the slash, from 0 to 32;
\(32 - p\) is how many bits are left to number the hosts;
\(U\) is the number of addresses you can use in an AWS subnet: AWS reserves 5 per subnet (the network address, .1 for the VPC router, .2 for DNS, .3 for future use, and the last address).

Example: the VPC 10.0.0.0/16 has \(N = 2^{32-16} = 2^{16} = 65{,}536\) addresses. The subnet 10.0.1.0/24 has \(N = 2^{8} = 256\) and \(U = 256 - 5 = 251\). LocalStack’s describe-subnets reported exactly AvailableIpAddressCount 251 for our new /24.
An address belongs to a block when their first \(p\) bits are equal. Writing addresses as 32-bit numbers (\(a.b.c.d \mapsto a \cdot 2^{24} + b \cdot 2^{16} + c \cdot 2^{8} + d\)):
\[
x \in B/p \iff \left\lfloor \frac{x}{2^{\,32-p}} \right\rfloor = \left\lfloor \frac{B}{2^{\,32-p}} \right\rfloor
\]
where:

\(x\) is the address being checked, as a number;
\(B/p\) is the block, with \(B\) its first address and \(p\) its prefix length;
dividing by \(2^{32-p}\) and rounding down with \(\lfloor\cdot\rfloor\) throws away the last \(32 - p\) bits, keeping only the first \(p\);
\(\iff\) reads “exactly when”.

Example: is web1 at 10.0.11.25 inside 10.0.0.0/16? As numbers, \(x = 10 \cdot 16{,}777{,}216 + 0 + 11 \cdot 256 + 25 = 167{,}775{,}001\) and \(B = 167{,}772{,}160\). With \(2^{16} = 65{,}536\): \(\lfloor 167{,}775{,}001 / 65{,}536 \rfloor = \lfloor 2560.04 \rfloor = 2560\) and \(\lfloor 167{,}772{,}160 / 65{,}536 \rfloor = 2560\). They are equal, so yes. For 10.1.0.5, \(x = 167{,}837{,}701\) and \(\lfloor x / 65{,}536 \rfloor = 2561 \neq 2560\), so no.
When several routes match a destination, the most specific (longest prefix) wins:
\[
r^{\ast}(x) = \operatorname*{arg\,max}_{\,r \in R \;:\; x \in r} \; p(r)
\]
where:

\(x\) is the destination address of a packet;
\(R\) is the set of routes in the subnet’s route table;
“\(r \in R : x \in r\)” keeps only the routes whose block contains \(x\);
\(p(r)\) is the prefix length of route \(r\);
\(\operatorname{arg\,max}\) returns the route with the largest \(p(r)\), not the number itself;
\(r^{\ast}(x)\) is the route the packet takes.

Example: the public table is \(R = \lbrace \texttt{10.0.0.0/16} \to \text{local},\ \texttt{0.0.0.0/0} \to \text{igw} \rbrace\). For $x = $ 10.0.11.25 both routes match, with \(p = 16\) and \(p = 0\); the larger is 16, so the packet stays local. For $x = $ 203.0.113.10 (an internet address) only 0.0.0.0/0 matches, so it goes to the internet gateway.
The NAT gateway that gives private subnets their updates is billed by the hour and by the gigabyte:
\[
C_{\text{NAT}} = r_h \cdot h + r_g \cdot g
\]
where:

\(C_{\text{NAT}}\) is the monthly cost in dollars;
\(r_h\) is the hourly rate, $0.05 per hour in London;
\(h\) is the hours it exists (730 for a whole month);
\(r_g\) is the data processing rate, $0.05 per GB in London;
\(g\) is the gigabytes that pass through it.

Example: one NAT gateway for a month, with 20 GB of updates: \(C_{\text{NAT}} = 0.05 \times 730 + 0.05 \times 20 = 36.50 + 1.00 = \$37.50\). That is more than both t3.micro web servers together ($17.23, lesson 1), which is why test accounts should delete it after use (prices at time of writing, November 2026).

Mathematical Abstraction
Why two AZs? Suppose each AZ is available a fraction \(a\) of the time and AZs fail independently. The stand is up when at least one AZ is up:
\[
A = 1 - (1 - a)^{n}
\]
where:

\(A\) is the availability of the whole stand, between 0 and 1;
\(a\) is the availability of one AZ;
\(1 - a\) is the chance one AZ is down, so \((1 - a)^n\) is the chance all \(n\) are down at once;
\(n\) is the number of AZs with a healthy server.

Example: take a deliberately pessimistic \(a = 0.99\), an illustrative number rather than an AWS figure. One AZ gives \(A = 0.99\), which means down \(0.01 \times 525{,}600 = 5{,}256\) minutes (about 3.7 days) a year. Two AZs give \(A = 1 - 0.01^2 = 0.9999\), down only \(0.0001 \times 525{,}600 \approx 53\) minutes a year. This is an idealised model. Real failures are not fully independent: a bad deployment or a regional problem can hit both AZs at once.
How fast does the ALB notice? With interval \(i\):
\[
T_{\text{remove}} = i \cdot u, \qquad T_{\text{return}} = i \cdot k
\]
where:

\(T_{\text{remove}}\) is the worst-case time until a broken target stops receiving customers, in seconds;
\(T_{\text{return}}\) is the time until a repaired target receives customers again;
\(i\) is the health-check interval in seconds;
\(u\) is the unhealthy threshold and \(k\) the healthy threshold, both counts of checks in a row.

Example: with the defaults \(i = 30\), \(u = 2\), \(k = 5\): \(T_{\text{remove}} = 30 \times 2 = 60\) s and \(T_{\text{return}} = 30 \times 5 = 150\) s. With \(i = 10\) and \(u = 2\) the stand drops a broken server after \(20\) s, at the price of three times as many checks.
Target tracking aims at the capacity that would bring the metric back to target, kept between the limits:
\[
d' = \min\!\Big(M,\ \max\!\Big(m,\ \Big\lceil d \cdot \frac{x}{t} \Big\rceil\Big)\Big)
\]
where:

\(d\) is the current desired capacity and \(d'\) the new one, in servers;
\(x\) is the current value of the metric (average CPU, in percent) and \(t\) is its target;
\(\lceil \cdot \rceil\) rounds up, because half a server is not possible;
\(m\) and \(M\) are the minimum and maximum sizes, so \(m \le d' \le M\) always holds.

Example: on Saturday, \(d = 2\) servers run at \(x = 90\%\) with target \(t = 50\%\). Then \(\lceil 2 \times 90 / 50 \rceil = \lceil 3.6 \rceil = 4\), and \(\min(6, \max(2, 4)) = 4\) servers. At night, \(d = 4\) at \(x = 10\%\) gives \(\lceil 4 \times 10/50 \rceil = \lceil 0.8 \rceil = 1\), but \(\max(2, 1) = 2\) keeps the minimum of two. This proportional rule is a simplified model. AWS’s real algorithm also waits for new instances to warm up and scales in more cautiously than it scales out.

Mathematical Abstraction
Durability says how many objects you should expect to lose:
\[
L = n \cdot (1 - d)
\]
where:

\(L\) is the expected number of objects lost per year;
\(n\) is the number of objects stored;
\(d\) is the designed annual durability, \(0.99999999999\), so \(1 - d = 10^{-11}\).

Example: AWS’s own illustration. With \(n = 10{,}000{,}000 = 10^{7}\) objects, \(L = 10^{7} \times 10^{-11} = 10^{-4}\) objects a year: on average one object every \(1 / 10^{-4} = 10{,}000\) years. The Lemonade bucket with \(n = 1{,}000\) objects gives \(L = 10^{3} \times 10^{-11} = 10^{-8}\), one object in 100 million years. Hardware is not the real risk; people deleting or overwriting files are. That is what versioning protects against. Eleven nines is a design target of a statistical model, not a guarantee in a contract.
With versioning, you pay for every version you keep:
\[
C = r \cdot \sum_{j=1}^{v} s_j
\]
where:

\(C\) is the monthly storage cost in dollars;
\(r\) is the price per GB-month ($0.024 for Standard in London);
\(v\) is the number of versions kept, and \(j\) numbers them;
\(s_j\) is the size of version \(j\) in GB.

Example: a 50 MB promotional video, re-uploaded every day. After 30 days \(v = 30\), so \(\sum s_j = 30 \times 0.05 = 1.5\) GB (taking 1 GB as 1,000 MB) and \(C = 0.024 \times 1.5 = \$0.036\) a month. After a year \(v = 365\): \(365 \times 0.05 = 18.25\) GB and \(C = \$0.44\). A lifecycle rule that deletes versions older than 30 days keeps \(v \le 31\), so the cost stays near $0.04 for good (price at time of writing, November 2026).
A presigned link works only inside its time window:
\[
\text{valid}(t) \iff t_0 \le t &lt; t_0 + e, \qquad e \le 604{,}800 \text{ s}
\]
where:

\(t\) is the moment someone uses the link;
\(t_0\) is the signing time, X-Amz-Date in the link;
\(e\) is the lifetime in seconds, X-Amz-Expires in the link;
\(604{,}800\) s is 7 days, the longest lifetime S3 accepts. Links signed with temporary credentials also stop when those credentials expire.

Example: our link has $t_0 = $ 14:23:32 UTC on 25 November 2026 and \(e = 300\) s, so it is valid until \(t_0 + 300\) s \(=\) 14:28:32 UTC. At 14:28:31 the supplier can download the menu. At 14:28:33 S3 answers 403 Request has expired.

Mathematical Abstraction
How much data can a failure lose? If orders arrive at a steady rate, the loss is at most
\[
L = \lambda \cdot \text{RPO}
\]
where:

\(L\) is the number of orders that could be lost;
\(\lambda\) (lambda) is the arrival rate, in orders per minute;
\(\text{RPO}\) (recovery point objective) is how far back the newest safe copy can be, in minutes.

Example: on a busy Saturday \(\lambda = 30\) orders per hour \(= 0.5\) per minute. Restoring from backups alone (logs every 5 minutes, so \(\text{RPO} \le 5\)) can lose up to \(L = 0.5 \times 5 = 2.5\), so at most 3 orders. With Multi-AZ the standby is written synchronously, so for a lost primary \(\text{RPO} = 0\) and \(L = 0.5 \times 0 = 0\). The customers wait 60–120 seconds during the failover, but no order disappears.
DynamoDB counts work in units. For an item of size \(s\) kilobytes:
\[
w = \left\lceil \frac{s}{1} \right\rceil, \qquad
r_{\text{strong}} = \left\lceil \frac{s}{4} \right\rceil, \qquad
r_{\text{eventual}} = \frac{1}{2} \left\lceil \frac{s}{4} \right\rceil
\]
where:

\(s\) is the item size in KB;
\(w\) is the write units for one write, charged per started 1 KB;
\(r_{\text{strong}}\) is the read units for one strongly consistent read, per started 4 KB;
\(r_{\text{eventual}}\) is the read units for one eventually consistent read, half as much;
\(\lceil \cdot \rceil\) rounds up, because a started block counts as a whole one.

Example: one Lemonade order item is \(s = 1.5\) KB. A write costs \(w = \lceil 1.5 \rceil = 2\) units. A strong read costs \(\lceil 1.5/4 \rceil = \lceil 0.375 \rceil = 1\) unit, and an eventual read \(0.5\) units.
The on-demand monthly bill is then
\[
C = W \cdot w \cdot p_w + R \cdot r \cdot p_r
\]
where:

\(C\) is the monthly cost in dollars;
\(W\) and \(R\) are the numbers of writes and reads in the month;
\(w\) and \(r\) are the units per write and per read from the formula above;
\(p_w\) and \(p_r\) are the prices per unit: in London $0.7423 and $0.1487 per million units (price at time of writing, November 2026).

Example: 100,000 orders written once and read twice (eventually consistent) in a month gives \(W = 100{,}000\), \(w = 2\), \(R = 200{,}000\), \(r = 0.5\). So \(C = 100{,}000 \times 2 \times 0.7423 \times 10^{-6} + 200{,}000 \times 0.5 \times 0.1487 \times 10^{-6} = 0.1485 + 0.0149 \approx \$0.16\). The first 25 GB of storage are free, so a small stand pays almost nothing.

The Lemonade Stand keeps an order log: one line per sale. A container’s own filesystem is thrown away whenever the container is replaced. A crash, a rolling update or a node drain would erase the day’s sales. We need storage that:

outlives the pod, so a new pod finds the old data;
can be requested by size (“1 GiB please”) without knowing which disk it lives on;
is created automatically when someone asks for it.

The Lemonade Stand keeps an order log: one line per sale. A container’s own filesystem is thrown away whenever the container is replaced. A crash, a rolling update or a node drain would erase the day’s sales. We need storage that:

outlives the pod, so a new pod finds the old data;
can be requested by size (“1 GiB please”) without knowing which disk it lives on;
is created automatically when someone asks for it.

The concept, precisely

A ConfigMap stores non-secret data as key–value pairs under data, or as binary under binaryData. A value can be a short string ("10:00-18:00") or a whole file (index.html). The limit is 1 MiB per object.
A Secret has the same shape, but:

values under data are base64-encoded. For convenience you can write plain text under stringData, and the API server encodes it for you;
it has a type: Opaque (general), kubernetes.io/tls (tls.crt + tls.key), kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson (registry login), kubernetes.io/basic-auth, and others;
access is normally restricted with RBAC, and kubectl describe hides the values;
it is also limited to 1 MiB.

Three ways to hand data to a pod:

one environment variable from one key: env[].valueFrom.configMapKeyRef or secretKeyRef;
all keys as environment variables: envFrom[].configMapRef or secretRef;
as files in a volume: volumes[].configMap or secret. Each key becomes a file.

How updates arrive:

Environment variables are read once, when the container starts. Changing the ConfigMap or Secret does not change them in running pods. Restart the pods (kubectl rollout restart).
Mounted volumes are refreshed by the kubelet after a delay of up to “kubelet sync period + cache delay”, typically under a minute or two. A volume mounted with subPath is never refreshed.
With immutable: true, the object can never change. That protects against mistakes and saves the API server from watching it.

k3s difference: start the servers with --secrets-encryption to encrypt Secrets inside the datastore. Without it they are stored base64-encoded only. k3s secrets-encrypt status shows the current state, and k3s secrets-encrypt rotate-keys rotates the key.

Mathematical Abstraction
Why does count reshuffle? Each copy’s address is its position:
\[
\operatorname{addr}_{C}(x_i) = i, \qquad \operatorname{addr}_{F}(x) = \operatorname{key}(x)
\]
where:

\(\operatorname{addr}_{C}\) is the address under count, and \(\operatorname{addr}_{F}\) the address under for_each;
\(x_0, x_1, \dots, x_{n-1}\) are the items of the list, \(n\) of them;
\(i\) is an item’s position, counting from 0;
\(\operatorname{key}(x)\) is the item’s name in the for_each map or set;
an “address” is how the state remembers the copy, such as local_file.menu[0] or docker_container.stand["web2"].

If you remove the item at position \(j\) with count, every later item moves down one position. Its address now points at different arguments, so it must change:
\[
\text{changed} = n - 1 - j, \qquad \text{destroyed} = 1
\]
where:

\(n\) is the length of the list before the removal;
\(j\) is the position of the removed item;
“changed” counts copies whose address now carries a different item (for a menu file, a replacement);
“destroyed” is the last address, which no longer exists.

Example: \(n = 3\) and we removed web1 at \(j = 0\), so changed \(= 3 - 1 - 0 = 2\) ([0] and [1] replaced) and destroyed \(= 1\) ([2]). Replacements count once in “add” and once in “destroy” (lesson 3): add \(= 2\), destroy \(= 2 + 1 = 3\). That is the real line Plan: 2 to add, 0 to change, 3 to destroy. Removing the last item instead (\(j = 2\)) gives changed \(= 3 - 1 - 2 = 0\), so only one destroy. With for_each the keys do not move, so removing any one stand always gives changed \(= 0\) and destroyed \(= 1\), as the real Docker plan shows.
cidrsubnet is integer arithmetic on IP addresses:
\[
\operatorname{cidrsubnet}(B/b,\ k,\ j) = \big(B + j \cdot 2^{\,32 - (b + k)}\big) / (b + k), \qquad 0 \le j &lt; 2^{k}
\]
where:

\(B/b\) is the big network: base address \(B\) with prefix length \(b\) (the first \(b\) bits are fixed);
\(k\) is newbits, how many extra bits the subnet fixes, so the new prefix is \(b + k\);
\(j\) is netnum, which subnet you want, counting from 0;
\(2^{\,32 - (b + k)}\) is the number of addresses in each subnet (an IPv4 address has 32 bits);
\(2^{k}\) is how many such subnets fit inside \(B/b\).

Example: the Lemonade VPC from Part 4 is \(10.0.0.0/16\), so \(b = 16\). With \(k = 8\) the new prefix is \(16 + 8 = 24\), and each subnet holds \(2^{32 - 24} = 2^{8} = 256\) addresses. For \(j = 1\) the start is \(10.0.0.0 + 1 \cdot 256 = 10.0.1.0\), giving 10.0.1.0/24, the real console result. \(j = 2\) gives \(10.0.0.0 + 512 = 10.0.2.0\), so 10.0.2.0/24 (also real). There are \(2^{8} = 256\) subnets, so \(j\) can go up to \(255\) (real result: 10.0.255.0/24), and \(j = 256\) fails with the real error prefix extension of 8 does not accommodate a subnet numbered 256.

Mathematical Abstraction
Lesson 1 described a plan as the gap between the desired set \(D\) and the recorded state \(S\). Terraform sorts every resource address into exactly one kind of action:
\[
\begin{aligned}
C &= D \setminus S && \text{create} \\
X &= S \setminus D && \text{destroy} \\
U &= \lbrace r \in D \cap S : d(r) \ne s(r),\ \text{the change can be made in place} \rbrace && \text{update} \\
P &= \lbrace r \in D \cap S : d(r) \ne s(r),\ \text{some changed argument forces replacement} \rbrace && \text{replace}
\end{aligned}
\]
where:

\(D\) is the set of resource addresses in the .tf files, and \(S\) is the set of addresses in the state (after refreshing it against reality);
\(\setminus\) is set difference (“in the first set but not the second”), and \(\cap\) is intersection (“in both”);
\(r\) is one resource address, such as local_file.menu;
\(d(r)\) is the resource’s arguments as written in the files, and \(s(r)\) is the arguments recorded in the state;
“forces replacement” is decided by the provider, per argument; a file’s content forces replacement, a tag on an EC2 instance does not.

The summary line then counts:
\[
\text{add} = \lvert C \rvert + \lvert P \rvert, \qquad \text{change} = \lvert U \rvert, \qquad \text{destroy} = \lvert X \rvert + \lvert P \rvert
\]
where each replacement in \(P\) counts once in “add” and once in “destroy”, because it is a destroy followed by a create.
Example, the price change in step 2. Let \(p\) stand for random_pet.stand and \(m\) for local_file.menu. Then \(D = S = \lbrace p, m \rbrace\), so \(C = X = \emptyset\). Only \(m\) changed (\(d(m) \ne s(m)\)), and its content forces replacement, so \(P = \lbrace m \rbrace\) and \(U = \emptyset\). Substituting: add \(= 0 + 1 = 1\), change \(= 0\), destroy \(= 0 + 1 = 1\), exactly the real line Plan: 1 to add, 0 to change, 1 to destroy. For two Docker containers whose content changes, \(\lvert P \rvert = 2\) gives add \(= 2\), destroy \(= 2\).
Scripts read the result through the exit code of plan -detailed-exitcode:
\[
e =
\begin{cases}
0 & \text{the plan succeeded and } C \cup X \cup U \cup P = \emptyset \\
1 & \text{an error occurred} \\
2 & \text{the plan succeeded and } C \cup X \cup U \cup P \ne \emptyset
\end{cases}
\]
where:

\(e\) is the number the shell sees in $?;
\(C \cup X \cup U \cup P\) is the set of all planned actions;
\(\emptyset\) is “no actions at all”.

Example: right after an apply, the plan is empty, so \(e = 0\) (real run: exit=0). After deleting menu.txt, \(C = \lbrace m \rbrace \ne \emptyset\), so \(e = 2\) (real run: exit=2). A nightly CI job can therefore alert whenever \(e = 2\): somebody changed the Lemonade Stand by hand.

Mathematical Abstraction
The pipeline applies only when every gate before it has passed:
\[
\text{apply} \Rightarrow f \land v \land p \land a
\]
where:

\(f\) is “terraform fmt -check exited 0”;
\(v\) is “terraform validate succeeded”;
\(p\) is “terraform plan -out succeeded”;
\(a\) is “a required reviewer approved the production environment”;
\(\land\) is “and”, and \(P \Rightarrow Q\) reads “if \(P\) happens, then \(Q\) was true”.

Example: a pull request with a messy file has \(f\) = false, so \(f \land v \land p \land a\) is false and apply can never run. A clean change that is merged but not yet approved has \(f = v = p\) = true and \(a\) = false, so the job waits. Only \(f = v = p = a\) = true lets it continue.
A saved plan carries the serial number of the state it was made from, and applies only against that state:
\[
\operatorname{apply}(P) \text{ is allowed} \iff \operatorname{serial}(S_{\text{now}}) = \operatorname{serial}(S_{P})
\]
where:

\(P\) is the saved plan file;
\(S_{P}\) is the state the plan was computed from, and \(S_{\text{now}}\) is the state at apply time;
\(\operatorname{serial}(S)\) is the counter Terraform increases every time it writes the state;
\(\iff\) means “exactly when”.

Example: the real run above had \(\operatorname{serial}(S_{P}) = 1\). The other apply replaced the file and raised the serial to \(\operatorname{serial}(S_{\text{now}}) = 4\). Since \(4 \ne 1\), apply was refused with Saved plan is stale, exactly as the rule predicts.
Why OIDC beats stored keys, in one ratio (a simplified model of exposure time):
\[
\rho = \frac{T_{\text{OIDC}}}{T_{\text{key}}}
\]
where:

\(T_{\text{OIDC}}\) is how long a leaked OIDC credential keeps working: the session lifetime, by default 1 hour for configure-aws-credentials;
\(T_{\text{key}}\) is how long a leaked static key keeps working: until it is rotated;
\(\rho\) is the fraction of the risk window that remains with OIDC.

Example: a team rotates keys every 90 days, so \(T_{\text{key}} = 90 \times 24 = 2160\) hours. With \(T_{\text{OIDC}} = 1\) hour, \(\rho = 1 / 2160 \approx 0.00046\): a leaked credential is useful to an attacker for less than one two-thousandth of the time. This ignores how fast a leak is noticed, but the direction is clear.

When not to make a module
HashiCorp’s own guide warns against modules that are only thin wrappers around one resource. A module is worth it when it:

groups several resources that always go together (a stand = container + menu + firewall rule);
hides a decision the caller should not need to make (which image, which ports);
is used in more than one place, or by more than one team.

It is not worth it when it wraps a single resource with the same arguments, when it is used exactly once, or when callers must pass twenty inputs to use it. Then it is just an extra file to open. Martin Fowler’s Refactoring passes on Don Roberts’s rule of three: copy once if you must, and extract the shared piece the third time you need it. Modules follow the same rule.

Mathematical Abstraction
A security-group rule is a membership test. A packet is allowed in exactly when its port is inside the rule’s range and its source is inside the rule’s address block:
\[
\operatorname{allow}(q, s) \iff \big(\ell \le q \le h\big) \land \big(s \in N\big)
\]
where:

\(q\) is the destination port of the incoming packet;
\(s\) is the source IP address;
\(\ell\) and \(h\) are the rule’s from_port and to_port;
\(N\) is the set of addresses in cidr_blocks; 0.0.0.0/0 is every IPv4 address;
\(\land\) means “and”, and \(\iff\) “exactly when”.

Example: a customer at \(s = 203.0.113.7\) opens the menu on port \(q = 80\). The rule has \(\ell = h = 80\), so \(80 \le 80 \le 80\) is true, and \(203.0.113.7 \in N\) because \(N\) is everything. The visit is allowed. The same customer trying SSH on \(q = 22\) fails the first test (\(80 \le 22\) is false), so the packet is dropped. That is exactly what you want for a lemonade menu.
Cost is where the two designs differ most. A VM is paid for every hour it is switched on. Cloud Run with request-based billing is paid only while it handles requests:
\[
C_{\text{VM}} = r \cdot H, \qquad
C_{\text{run}} = n \, t \, (c \, p_{c} + m \, p_{m}) + \frac{n}{10^{6}} \, p_{r}
\]
where:

\(C_{\text{VM}}\) and \(C_{\text{run}}\) are the monthly costs in US dollars;
\(r\) is the VM’s hourly price and \(H\) the hours per month it runs (about \(730\) for always-on);
\(n\) is the number of requests per month, and \(t\) the seconds each request keeps the container busy;
\(c\) is vCPUs and \(m\) is GiB of memory per container;
\(p_{c}\), \(p_{m}\), \(p_{r}\) are the prices per vCPU-second, per GiB-second and per million requests.

Example, with the London t3.micro price from Part 4 and the Cloud Run Tier 1 list prices from Part 5, both at the time of writing (check the live pricing pages, because prices and free tiers change): \(r = 0.0118\), so \(C_{\text{VM}} = 0.0118 \times 730 \approx 8.61\) dollars, even if nobody buys lemonade. For Cloud Run take \(n = 300{,}000\) requests (10,000 a day), \(t = 0.2\) s, \(c = 1\), \(m = 0.5\), \(p_{c} = 0.000024\), \(p_{m} = 0.0000025\) and \(p_{r} = 0.40\):

busy seconds: \(n \, t = 300{,}000 \times 0.2 = 60{,}000\);
CPU: \(60{,}000 \times 1 \times 0.000024 = 1.44\);
memory: \(60{,}000 \times 0.5 \times 0.0000025 = 0.075\);
requests: \(0.3 \times 0.40 = 0.12\);
total: \(C_{\text{run}} \approx 1.44 + 0.075 + 0.12 \approx 1.64\) dollars, before the monthly free tier, which at this traffic would cover it entirely.

This is a simplified upper-bound model. It assumes each request is handled alone; when one container serves several requests at once, the busy time is shared and the real bill is lower. It also ignores data transfer and the first-boot time of the VM.
